Chapter: GopWebSpawn
  cites Works chapter GopWeb

 THE FIRST SHIPPED CALLER OF `process-spawn`, graded. Everything this arm
 asks about is the SPAWN, not the webserver: whether a chapter outside
 `codex/test` can put a service in a process of its own, whether that
 process is alive and running its body after the parent has carried on,
 and whether the parent survives doing so.

 The parent yields in its hold, so a child that lands on the same core runs
 in the gaps; `process-status` above zero after the hold is the reading. THE SABOTAGE THIS ARM EXISTS FOR: delete the
 `gopweb-start` line and the spawned line and the alive line both turn
 False. An arm that still reports a live service without the spawn is
 measuring the parent.

Section: Body

  spin : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  spin (n) (acc) = if n == 0 then acc else spin (n - 1) (acc + 1)

  hold-yielding : Integer -> Integer
  hold-yielding (rounds) =
    if rounds <= 0 then 0
    else let y = process-yield
    in let s = spin 10000000 0
    in if s < 0 then 0 else hold-yielding (rounds - 1)

 THE ROW ON THIS LINE IS ITSELF AN ASSERTION AND MUST NOT BE WIDENED. The
 service runs under `Network.Read` and `Network.Write`; this chapter spawns it
 and declares neither. `process-spawn` takes its argument's effect
 row under a `ForAllEff` and answers `Concurrent` alone, so a child's effects
 do not reach whoever spawned it, and this row compiling is what demonstrates
 that rather than asserting it. Adding `Network.*` back would still compile
 and would silently destroy the evidence.

  opening : [Console, Concurrent, Capability] Nothing = act
    pid <- gopweb-start gopweb-blk-new
    print-line-uni ("spawned " & show (pid > 0))
    let held = hold-yielding 200
    in act
      print-line-uni ("parent still running " & show (spin 1000 0 == 1000))
      print-line-uni ("service alive " & show (process-status pid > 0))
      k <- process-kill pid
      print-line-uni ("service stopped " & show (k >= 0))
    end
  end
